welcome everyone to another coffee break  here on my channel firm learning my name  is heinrich i'm a former mckinsey  consultant and on my channel i talk  about how to become successful in the  first years of your career and today  let's look into what steve jobs had to  say about consulting so of course if  steve says something we all better  listen steve surely is a great and  intelligent man and i found i think one  of the less known videos of steve jobs  where he commented on his perspective of  consulting the videos from a speech he  gave at mit where he essentially shared  two points on consulting so let's look  at them i think part of what he says is  correct but i do also think that this  view is at least partially flawed so  let's get into it how many of you are  from manufacturing companies oh  excellent where where are the rest you  from  how many from consulting  oh that's bad  you should do something  no seriously i i don't think there's  anything inherently evil in consulting i  think that  i think that without  without owning something  over an extended period of time like a  few years  where one has a chance to take  responsibility for one's recommendations  where one has to see one's  recommendations through all action  stages and accumulate scar tissue for  the mistakes and pick oneself up off the  ground and dust oneself off  one learns a fraction  of what one can what you're  coming in and making recommendations and  not  owning  the results not owning the  implementation  um  i think is  is uh  a fraction of the of the value and a  fraction of the opportunity to learn and  get better  so here steve jobs is essentially saying  that consultants do not own the products  of their work and do not follow through  with it in the execution but i think  it's fair to say that consultants of  course own the concepts and strategies  that they might develop especially maybe  consulting so mckinsey bc bane surely  it's correct that at least in most cases  you do not witness the execution of  these concepts and surely also not  responsible for them so the one side of  the story is that he is correct and i  did also witness sometimes even up to a  point where i thought that this was a  little bit arrogant that there was often  a little bit of the notion that actually  developing the concepts and the  strategies this is what the challenging  part is and then the execution that  wouldn't be that tough that difficult  and this is also the context in which i  released a video a couple of weeks ago  about the boston consulting group and  gamestop legal dispute where boston  consulting group was suing gamestop for  about 30 million dollar in consulting  fees if you haven't seen that i will  link it somewhere above here but this  video this whole topic was also very  much linked to the fact the boston  consulting group actually was not  responsible at least from their  perspective for the actual  implementation of the measures that  they're recommended and there seem to be  lots of disputes surrounding that so  this criticism the consultants do not  really own their ideas from end to end  from the very start where you  conceptualize them until they are  financially effective this is of course  a common criticism though here i often  wonder whether that's really a fair  point to make because let's look at  other roles in corporate let's look at  salespeople do they own everything of  the value chain of what happens in their  company no they are just sales people  they're selling products and you could  argue this is also only very limited  part of what happens in a company what  about accountants financial accountants  managerial accountants people working in  a controlling team do they really own  the company processes from end to end  well i'd argue they're very efficient  and capable in a certain area what if  you are a software engineer so you  certainly build something and are  responsible for the final product of  your work but here also are you really  owning it end to end here someone else  will usually conceptualize and define  the things that then you are supposed to  build so we get very detailed  descriptions usually from the product  team telling you what to do and then you  do just that is this really end-to-end  ownership now you could continue talking  about engineers working in rd or people  working in production i'd say that in  almost every role in a corporate  environment you are only responsible for  a certain part of the whole but not  everything and in this same way the  strategy consigns are responsible for  developing the strategy concepts and of  course many other things depending on  the types of projects they're working on  and which is not that different from  many of these other roads this way and  so  what what you you do get a broad cut at  companies but it's very thin  it's like a picture of uh i would i  could use i'm a vegetarian so won't you  steak it's like a picture of a  of a banana  it you might get a very accurate picture  but it's only two-dimensional  and without the experience of actually  doing it  you never get three-dimensional so you  might have a lot of pictures on your  walls you can show it off to your  friends you can say look i've worked in  bananas i've worked in peaches i've  worked in grapes but you never really  taste it  and and i think that that um that's  that's the right thing  so this point builds on the notion that  consultants don't really understand what  they're talking about that at best they  have a very superficial knowledge of the  things that they express their opinions  on and here again in favor of steve jobs  of course as a consultant you will  rarely have the knowledge that someone  has who's really working in an  operational way on a certain topic for  years and years and years of course such  a person will likely be more  knowledgeabilities on some parts of the  things than you as a consultant in  addition to that i do think it's true  also from my personal experience that  sometimes there's the problem that is  consultant you need to be perceived as  competent and you need to do things to  some extent fake this competency even  though you're still learning you're  still in the process of acquiring all  the knowledge that you need to acquire  there was a harvard business review  article a couple of years ago that  called this the learning credibility  tension that's a tension that many  people and professional services face so  it's not only specific to consultant but  a very relevant interesting topic indeed  i created a video about that a couple of  months ago i will also link it above  here with some very interesting insights  on that check it out if this topic  interests you but that being said i  still think that this notion that  consultants don't really know what  they're talking about in the end is  still very much flawed this way from my  perspective it's actually one of the  biggest advantages of having worked  several years in consulting that after  this time you will be able to have a  meaningful conversation with any senior  leader of most companies independent  from the industry or from the function  that the leader is working in just based  on your insights and based on your  contextual understanding of what the key  topics are that matter to these type of  people and of course also what they mean  on an execution operational level  because in the end of the day these are  things that you will at least have some  exposure on in your work as a consult in  this way on top of that the big variety  of different topics you will work on at  least in mbb consulting really enables  you to if then you need to go deep in a  certain part of your career also after  consulting to give you the confidence  that you can understand also complex  things in a short amount of time and  give you the ability to really amass  lots of knowledge in short time to get  this done and to make yourself  knowledgeable whenever this is than  needed so in the end of the day to me  this comes back to the old debate  whether being a generalist is better or  whether being a specialist is better in  my humble opinion there are very good  arguments to be made that especially in  the beginning of your career it makes a  lot of sense to become a generalist  first you will still have plenty of  opportunities later down in your career  to amass niche knowledge to mass  knowledge in a very specific domain to  become this very t-shaped profile  whereas on the one hand side a very  broad general understanding of things  and that also is able to go deep and has  the main expertise in some specific  areas that often makes a lot of sense  because if you specialize yourself too  early on in your career but i understand  that the rewards for that can be  significant as well then often it is  difficult then after years and years and  years in this specific role you then  want to do things which are very very  different from that but now i'm very  interested to hear your opinion as well  do you actually agree with what steve  jobs said or not just leave a comment  below in the comment section very happy  to have a conversation with you and as  always if you took any value from this  video at all destroy the like button for  the youtube algorithm and also subscribe  to stay up to date my name is heinrich  big big thanks to all the members of  your channel for your support are these  videos every single saturday see you  again next week until then all the best  to you and bye bye

